/target
.DS_Store
node_modules
# static - bun build outputs there, populated at run/deploy time
/static
# real secrets (the cloudflare tunnel token lives here). .env.example
# is the tracked template, .env never is.
/.env
.env.local
# runtime checks - local scratch tests, not part of the shipped app.
# they are run with `bun run <file>.test.tsx` and are never deployed.
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test.tsx

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins the ACTION SHAPE the app hands to the wallet, and the
# shape decides whether a FastFS upload works at all. the wallet reads
# the arguments on its own side, in another language, and when the two
# sides disagree the upload is DROPPED SILENTLY: the receipt fails the
# way a FastFS receipt always fails, the file never appears, and nothing
# says why. that has happened twice. a test that catches the mismatch
# is not scratch — it is the thing that keeps the two sides in step.
!frontend/lib/ts/shared/queue_args_wallet_action.test.ts

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins the ORDER of the keys page's list and the shape of each
# row. two rules hang on it: the FULL ACCESS key comes first, and only a
# LIMITED key is pickable. the full access key IS the account — it can
# do anything the account can do — so removing it locks the reader out
# of their own account, and it is very often the key the wallet itself
# signs with. a test that catches the list putting it in the wrong
# place, or making it pickable, is not scratch.
!frontend/lib/ts/keys/app_page_keys_types.test.ts

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins the WALLET ACTION SHAPE the keys page hands to the
# wallet for an AddKey and a DeleteKey. the shape decides whether the
# reader SEES what they are approving: near-connect hands an action
# through untouched when it already carries a `type`, and near-kit
# produces exactly such an action with the BORSH permission where the
# connector wants the flat one. the wallet then cannot say what the key
# is for and shows NO TRANSACTION PREVIEW — the transaction still goes
# out, silently, unread. a test that catches the shape drifting is not
# scratch — it is the thing that keeps the reader's approval meaningful.
!frontend/lib/ts/keys/app_page_keys_wallet_action.test.ts

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins the ACCOUNT ID RULE and the URL parameters of the keys
# page's form. the account a key may call does NOT have to be a
# contract, so the app asks the chain nothing about it — it asks one
# local thing: is this string an account id the chain will accept. the
# test also pins that the url can prefill the form, so a link to the
# page can say what the key is for.
!frontend/lib/ts/keys/app_page_keys_account_id.test.ts

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins the ALLOWANCE of a limited key: what the reader types
# in NEAR, what the chain stores in yoctoNEAR, and what the reader sees
# back. the one rule that matters most is in there — the human figure
# must NEVER round a real limit down to "0", because "the key can spend
# nothing" is a different statement from "the key can spend one
# yoctoNEAR".
!frontend/lib/ts/keys/app_page_keys_allowance.test.ts

# --- WHY THIS ONE IS TRACKED ---
# this test pins that the keys list is rebuilt FROM THE CHAIN after
# every add and every remove. the chain is the only thing that knows
# what the account has, so a store that patched its own data would show
# the reader a key the chain never accepted. it also pins that a full
# access key can NEVER be picked for removal.
!frontend/lib/ts/keys/app_page_keys_store.test.ts
/.openchamber
